# Citation File Format (CFF) -- https://citation-file-format.github.io
# Reference managers (Zotero, Mendeley) and GitHub's "Cite this repository"
# button auto-extract from this file.
#
# `preferred-citation:` points to the arXiv preprint (paper + supplement),
# arXiv:2610.28517. The top-level `doi:` field is intentionally blank until
# the Zenodo archive (code + data + monograph) is deposited; it then takes
# that archive's DOI. The schema below is otherwise complete.

cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Pulse Reliability Atlas"
message: "If you use this software or its outputs, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Nanini
    given-names: Santino
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0000-5645-4691"
repository-code: "https://github.com/sadc-lab/pulse_reliability_atlas"
license: Apache-2.0
version: "0.1.0"
date-released: 2026-05-22
keywords:
  - pulse-physiology-engine
  - sensitivity-analysis
  - morris-elementary-effects
  - reliability-atlas
  - ards
  - virtual-patient
  - reproducibility
abstract: >-
  The first open reliability atlas for the Pulse Physiology Engine
  (Kitware, v4.3.1, Apache-2.0). Characterises the engine's
  user-facing operational envelope (HR, BP, RR, Vt, FiO2, PEEP) via a
  preregistered 1,280-run Morris elementary-effects sweep across two
  adult reference surrogates and four scenarios (healthy + three
  ARDS severities). Reports H1-H4 hypothesis verdicts, a four-flag
  failure-mode classifier, two structural findings (F-003 transient
  hyperoxia regime, F-004 VC-AC patient-ventilator dyssynchrony with
  an operator-actionable trigger-pressure fix), a sex-stratified
  finding (solver divergence was all-male in an input-extreme regime,
  20/1,280 runs; reducing the male blood-volume baseline to the
  4,082 mL floor eliminated it), and a per-cell traffic-light curation
  and review-priority map.
